true"></span><span><span class="nm">O Come, All Ye Faithful</span><span class="tp">1751 · English Catholics; later Christians of many churches</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/joy-to-the-world"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Joy to the World</span><span class="tp">1719 · English and American Protestant hymn singing</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/silent-night"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Silent Night</span><span class="tp">1818 · Catholic parish of Oberndorf, Salzburg; later Christians worldwide</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/good-king-wenceslas"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Good King Wenceslas</span><span class="tp">c. 1853 · Victorian Anglicans of the Oxford Movement; English-speaking carol singers</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/the-holly-and-the-ivy"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">The Holly and the Ivy</span><span class="tp">c. 1823 · the country people and townspeople of England who kept and sang folk carols</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/hark-the-herald-angels-sing"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing</span><span class="tp">1739 · the early Methodists of England; later Anglican and other English-speaking churches</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/the-first-nowell"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">The First Nowell</span><span class="tp">1823 · the people of Cornwall; later English church choirs and carol singers</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/the-twelve-days-of-christmas"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">The Twelve Days of Christmas</span><span class="tp">1780 · English children and families, who played it as a memory-and-forfeits game at Christmas</span></span></a></li><l