e Second World War years</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/god-rest-ye-merry-gentlemen"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen</span><span class="tp">c. 1650 · English</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/o-tannenbaum"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">O Tannenbaum</span><span class="tp">c. 1824 · German</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/o-holy-night"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">O Holy Night</span><span class="tp">1847 · French Catholics; later Christians of Canada, the United States and beyond</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/jingle-bells"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Jingle Bells</span><span class="tp">1857 · American popular song; later Christmas celebrations around the world</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/shchedryk"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Shchedryk</span><span class="tp">1916 · Ukrainians (New Year well-wishing song, shchedrivka)</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/maoz-tzur"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Ma'oz Tzur</span><span class="tp">c. 1200 · Jewish communities, first those of Germany</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne"><span class="ph ph-work" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Auld Lang Syne</span><span class="tp">1788 · Scots</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
